  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Isotorachrysone may interact with SCCH-26 through multiple pathways. The first pathway links Isotorachrysone to Ataxin-2, which is subsequently targeted by a complex involving the (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one. This complex then targets SCCH-26. The second pathway connects Isotorachrysone to 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which is also targeted by the same complex and ultimately leads to SCCH-26. A third pathway shows that Isotorachrysone targets 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which is targeted by WS-5995 B, which in turn targets SCCH-26. These findings suggest that Isotorachrysone may act as a modulator of the proteasome pathway, influencing SCCH-26 through multiple intermediate targets. The evidence is consistent across different pathways, indicating a potential role for Isotorachrysone in modulating the proteasome system, which could have implications for cancer treatment. The strength of the evidence is moderate, with some pathways showing similar scores, but the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated."
